              text: 'Like other organic molecules, numbering of carbon atoms begins from the carboxylic group and carboxylic carbon being C-1 and α-carbon would be C-2 (Fig. 3.22). Electrochemical properties of amino acids At physiological pH (pH=7) the α-COOH group and α-NH group of amino acids are ionized (deprotonated) in solutions Fig. 3.21: Structure of negatively charged amino acids CO O – C CH H HN + CO O – CO O – C CH H HN + CH CO O – Aspartate Glutamate Fig. 3.20: Structure of aromatic amino acids CH CH Fig. 3.22: Numbering of carbon atoms of L-lysine Carbohydrade Final 30.018.2018.indd 09/01/2025 15:22:39 B iomolecules to form –COO - (bearing negative charge) and the – + NH (bearing a positive charge), respectively. This dipolar state of amino acids is called zwitterion. Zwitterions state occurs as a result of migration of proton from carboxyl group to amino group (Fig. 3.23). Non-standard and non-protein amino acids Some amino acids are non-standard amino acids, which occur naturally in the cells but do not take part in protein synthesis. They are generated after protein synthesis by modification of the specific standard amino acids. Examples are 4-hydroxyproline (formed by hydroxylation of proline), 5-hydroxylysine, selenocysteine and γ -carboxyglutamic acid. Amino acids that are not part of proteins are widely present in various plants, animals, and microbes known as non-protein amino acids. Some non-protein amino acids are L-ornithine, L-citrulline, β-alanine, creatine, and γ -aminobutyrate. 3.4 p rotein s tru C ture Proteins are one of the macromolecules present in all cells from simplest bacteria to human beings and plants. A single cell may contain thousands of different proteins.',
