              text: 'Proteins are made up of naturally occurring amino acids, which are linked covalently to form a linear sequence. The most remarkable thing is that cells can produce proteins with entirely different structures and properties by joining the amino acids in many different combinations. From these building blocks, the organisms can make vast variety of products such as catalysts (enzymes), antibodies, transporters, hormones, transcription factors, muscle fibres, membrane proteins etc. These products participate in all vital processes such as, in the transport of oxygen and nutrients to each cell, muscle contraction, transmission of nerve impulse, control of metabolism, growth and differentiation, providing mechanical support, immune response, signal transduction and many more. Composition of amino acids and their order in proteins decide the structure of a protein. Four levels of protein structures exist namely primary, secondary, tertiary, and quaternary, which are described below. Fig. 3.23: Zwitterion of glycine Carbohydrade Final 30.018.2018.indd 09/01/2025 15:22:39 B iotechnology 3.4.1 Primary structure of proteins The primary structure of proteins is the linear chain of amino acid sequences that are linked through peptide bonds (Fig. 3.24). The peptide bond (also called amide bond) is formed by coupling of the α-carboxyl group of one amino acid to an α-amino group of another amino acid. Formation of a peptide bond between two amino acids is accompanied by the loss of water molecules. Each amino acid unit in a polypeptide is therefore called a residue. On the basis of number of amino acids constituting a chain, the peptides may be called as a dipeptide (containing two amino acid units), a tripeptide (containing three amino Fig.',
